1
00:00:00,192 --> 00:00:03,453
One core API built into Bun that makes Bun

2
00:00:03,629 --> 00:00:06,504
very helpful and useful for building both

3
00:00:06,841 --> 00:00:08,062
web apps, websites,

4
00:00:08,688 --> 00:00:11,082
and utility tools, CLI tools,

5
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:13,330
is its built-in image

6
00:00:13,908 --> 00:00:15,418
API. Because Bun

7
00:00:15,996 --> 00:00:19,530
has a built-in feature, a built-in API which

8
00:00:19,546 --> 00:00:22,164
works without adding any extra packages

9
00:00:22,983 --> 00:00:26,099
that allows you to analyze and transform

10
00:00:26,452 --> 00:00:29,787
image files, for example, to optimize image

11
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:32,369
files served by your website, something

12
00:00:32,385 --> 00:00:34,085
we'll dive in in the next lecture.

13
00:00:34,791 --> 00:00:36,555
But in this lecture, we'll take a look at

14
00:00:36,619 --> 00:00:39,987
how the API in general works. And therefore

15
00:00:39,987 --> 00:00:41,992
I have a brand new project which you find

16
00:00:41,992 --> 00:00:43,948
attached. And in that project, I have some

17
00:00:44,012 --> 00:00:47,011
dummy images in an input folder. These are

18
00:00:47,284 --> 00:00:50,764
AI-generated nature photos and these images

19
00:00:50,972 --> 00:00:53,330
aren't large to be honest, but still you

20
00:00:53,330 --> 00:00:55,591
might wanna optimize them and maybe you also

21
00:00:55,639 --> 00:00:57,884
wanna transform them. For example, turn this

22
00:00:57,884 --> 00:01:01,446
PNG file into a JPEG file to be smaller for

23
00:01:01,494 --> 00:01:03,323
the purposes you wanna use it for.

24
00:01:03,884 --> 00:01:06,675
And to achieve this, this demo program has

25
00:01:06,852 --> 00:01:09,081
one source code file, the index.ts file.

26
00:01:09,675 --> 00:01:12,851
Now, in this file, I first find the images

27
00:01:12,948 --> 00:01:15,049
by reading the input directory, which is

28
00:01:15,049 --> 00:01:17,744
hardcoded to input here. I then filter to

29
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:18,145
only

30
00:01:18,722 --> 00:01:22,268
handle image files that end with.jpeg, with

31
00:01:22,444 --> 00:01:23,567
E or without E, and

32
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:28,620
e_or.png. And then we go through all these

33
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:30,400
identified image files and call

34
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,520
optimizeImage on each of them. And

35
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:34,760
optimizeImage is this function where we now

36
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:37,600
use the Bun Image API. Well, first I

37
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:39,940
construct an absolute path to the image, I

38
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:41,920
get the base name, so the part in front of

39
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:44,340
the extension, and I then define my output

40
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:46,600
path, which is basically the input file name

41
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,460
with.optimized and then always.jpeg

42
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:52,360
because I'll convert all images to JPEG here

43
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:54,444
in this example. We also then have a

44
00:01:54,444 --> 00:01:58,950
placeholder.txt file and an HTML file. I'll

45
00:01:58,950 --> 00:02:01,130
get back to the placeholder. The HTML file

46
00:02:01,259 --> 00:02:04,033
will just be some demo HTML file that shows

47
00:02:04,305 --> 00:02:05,588
the optimized image later.

48
00:02:06,534 --> 00:02:09,469
But then we use the actual Bun Image API.

49
00:02:10,062 --> 00:02:12,435
And there are 2 ways of getting started with

50
00:02:12,467 --> 00:02:13,205
it, so to say.

51
00:02:13,830 --> 00:02:16,652
Here is the first approach, which is based

52
00:02:16,652 --> 00:02:18,096
on the Bun File API.

53
00:02:18,721 --> 00:02:20,469
When you load a file with Bun,

54
00:02:21,304 --> 00:02:23,826
you can call the image method on that file

55
00:02:24,034 --> 00:02:27,135
to get an image object on which you can then

56
00:02:27,167 --> 00:02:30,026
perform resize and transformation

57
00:02:30,283 --> 00:02:32,516
operations. And of course, that will only

58
00:02:32,613 --> 00:02:34,829
work if you loaded a file with the Bun File

59
00:02:34,958 --> 00:02:35,231
API.

60
00:02:35,761 --> 00:02:38,363
If you loaded a file with the Node.js API

61
00:02:38,476 --> 00:02:40,484
with readFile, for example, it will not

62
00:02:40,484 --> 00:02:42,122
work, of course, because this is a Bun

63
00:02:42,122 --> 00:02:42,588
feature.

64
00:02:43,552 --> 00:02:46,299
Alternatively, you can create such an image

65
00:02:46,508 --> 00:02:49,319
object by instantiating BunImage like this.

66
00:02:50,041 --> 00:02:52,547
And then to that constructor function, you

67
00:02:52,547 --> 00:02:55,887
can pass your image input path. So the path

68
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:57,975
to the image file you wanna operate on.

69
00:02:58,408 --> 00:03:01,813
Alternatively, the BunImage constructor also

70
00:03:01,861 --> 00:03:05,346
takes raw bytes or a blob object. So if you

71
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:08,381
get an image from an incoming HTTP request,

72
00:03:08,510 --> 00:03:09,104
for example.

73
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:11,063
Now on this image

74
00:03:11,641 --> 00:03:13,488
object, you can then perform various

75
00:03:13,568 --> 00:03:15,287
operations as you see here.

76
00:03:15,913 --> 00:03:18,065
For example, you can call the metadata

77
00:03:18,305 --> 00:03:20,858
method to extract some metadata information.

78
00:03:21,597 --> 00:03:23,604
This gives you an object which at the point

79
00:03:23,604 --> 00:03:26,141
of time where I'm recording this has 3

80
00:03:26,270 --> 00:03:28,887
properties, width, height, and format.

81
00:03:29,497 --> 00:03:31,954
So here you can analyze an image. But the

82
00:03:32,034 --> 00:03:33,848
interesting part starts down here.

83
00:03:34,603 --> 00:03:36,995
Here we use this image object to, for

84
00:03:37,027 --> 00:03:39,773
example, resize it, convert it to a JPEG,

85
00:03:39,837 --> 00:03:40,608
and then save it.

86
00:03:41,442 --> 00:03:45,114
Now resize takes a width and a height. These

87
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:47,599
are these 2 values, first width, then the

88
00:03:47,599 --> 00:03:47,952
height,

89
00:03:48,802 --> 00:03:51,848
and an optional configuration object in

90
00:03:51,944 --> 00:03:53,692
there. For example, I'm setting without

91
00:03:53,692 --> 00:03:56,353
enlargement to make sure that if I have a

92
00:03:56,353 --> 00:03:59,143
higher width and/or height than the input

93
00:03:59,143 --> 00:04:01,997
image, we don't increase the size or the

94
00:04:02,045 --> 00:04:03,825
width and height of the image. Because of

95
00:04:03,873 --> 00:04:05,653
course, if you're transforming an image to

96
00:04:05,653 --> 00:04:07,865
be wider or taller than the input image,

97
00:04:08,362 --> 00:04:09,805
that will typically lead to quality loss.

98
00:04:09,870 --> 00:04:12,421
Quality loss because this is not some AI

99
00:04:12,470 --> 00:04:15,439
feature that generates a new image. Instead,

100
00:04:15,455 --> 00:04:17,252
it operates on the existing image and

101
00:04:17,509 --> 00:04:19,820
enlarging images will pretty much always

102
00:04:19,997 --> 00:04:22,677
give you quality loss. So that's why I like

103
00:04:22,677 --> 00:04:23,624
to set this to true.

104
00:04:24,652 --> 00:04:27,958
You can also set fit to control whether the

105
00:04:27,958 --> 00:04:30,606
image will be stretched if the aspect ratio

106
00:04:30,751 --> 00:04:31,521
you define here

107
00:04:32,131 --> 00:04:34,859
deviates from the input image aspect ratio.

108
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:38,167
With fit set to inside, the image will not

109
00:04:38,167 --> 00:04:40,945
stretch. So if you have a different aspect

110
00:04:40,945 --> 00:04:43,594
ratio here than the input image has, the

111
00:04:43,610 --> 00:04:45,906
original aspect ratio will be maintained,

112
00:04:46,099 --> 00:04:48,025
which means your width and height will not

113
00:04:48,025 --> 00:04:48,989
be hit perfectly.

114
00:04:49,711 --> 00:04:51,349
If you set fit to fill,

115
00:04:51,943 --> 00:04:54,817
then the image will be stretched to meet

116
00:04:54,914 --> 00:04:57,403
this aspect ratio. Simply depends on what

117
00:04:57,403 --> 00:04:59,827
you want. Typically, you probably want

118
00:04:59,827 --> 00:05:00,710
inside though.

119
00:05:01,899 --> 00:05:04,918
Now this will resize the image, but I also

120
00:05:04,966 --> 00:05:07,231
wanna convert it to a JPEG. Even if the

121
00:05:07,231 --> 00:05:09,511
input image was a PNG, I wanna get a JPEG as

122
00:05:09,543 --> 00:05:11,952
an output. That's why we can call the JPEG

123
00:05:11,952 --> 00:05:14,378
method. And there are other image format

124
00:05:14,426 --> 00:05:15,951
methods too, like AWIF

125
00:05:16,594 --> 00:05:20,063
or PNG if you want a PNG or WebP if you want

126
00:05:20,063 --> 00:05:22,938
a WebP image as an output. But I want JPEG.

127
00:05:23,516 --> 00:05:24,801
And depending on which format you're

128
00:05:24,801 --> 00:05:26,712
targeting, you can pass a configuration

129
00:05:26,792 --> 00:05:29,600
object with format-specific options. For

130
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,198
example, for a JPEG file, you can set the

131
00:05:32,246 --> 00:05:35,229
quality level so that we can shrink the file

132
00:05:35,229 --> 00:05:35,726
size a bit.

133
00:05:36,656 --> 00:05:39,447
But up to this point, nothing will have

134
00:05:39,463 --> 00:05:41,628
happened yet. Instead, what we're doing here

135
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:44,418
is we're setting up an image transformation

136
00:05:44,530 --> 00:05:46,599
pipeline. That's how Bun works. We're

137
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:48,668
operating on the image, we're resizing it,

138
00:05:49,005 --> 00:05:50,705
we're converting it to a JPEG, but we're

139
00:05:50,705 --> 00:05:53,431
just planning these steps, so to say.

140
00:05:54,218 --> 00:05:56,867
Only when you call a so-called terminal

141
00:05:56,867 --> 00:05:59,918
method like write, only then will the

142
00:05:59,918 --> 00:06:00,656
pipeline run.

143
00:06:01,523 --> 00:06:03,723
Other terminal methods are blob, for

144
00:06:03,771 --> 00:06:06,003
example, to get a blob which you could send

145
00:06:06,035 --> 00:06:10,178
with an HTTP response or to base64 to get

146
00:06:10,210 --> 00:06:12,105
the image as a base64 string.

147
00:06:12,763 --> 00:06:13,823
But I want write

148
00:06:14,609 --> 00:06:17,034
and write then simply takes a path where the

149
00:06:17,034 --> 00:06:19,426
transformed image will be saved to. And

150
00:06:19,523 --> 00:06:21,336
that's when that image pipeline will run.

151
00:06:21,834 --> 00:06:23,904
write then returns a promise because it's an

152
00:06:24,097 --> 00:06:26,215
asynchronous operation. Running this entire

153
00:06:26,264 --> 00:06:28,992
pipeline is an asynchronous operation, so we

154
00:06:28,992 --> 00:06:29,795
should await it.

155
00:06:30,677 --> 00:06:32,363
But I then have another step here in the

156
00:06:32,363 --> 00:06:35,155
next line. I call placeholder on the same

157
00:06:35,252 --> 00:06:38,028
image as before. Now, very important, resize

158
00:06:38,141 --> 00:06:38,863
and JPEG,

159
00:06:39,409 --> 00:06:42,426
they change the original image object or

160
00:06:42,474 --> 00:06:44,448
they set up the pipeline, but we're always

161
00:06:44,625 --> 00:06:46,776
operating on the same shared image object.

162
00:06:47,048 --> 00:06:49,840
So down here, I also operate on an image

163
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:52,150
object which has these changes already

164
00:06:52,262 --> 00:06:52,567
planned.

165
00:06:53,609 --> 00:06:56,352
And placeholder is then another terminal

166
00:06:56,352 --> 00:06:58,357
method that kicks off the pipeline. In this

167
00:06:58,357 --> 00:07:00,763
case, again, since we already ran it, but

168
00:07:00,763 --> 00:07:02,575
that's no problem. You can run a pipeline

169
00:07:02,607 --> 00:07:05,222
multiple times. Here I'm running it again to

170
00:07:05,238 --> 00:07:06,986
generate a placeholder image.

171
00:07:07,579 --> 00:07:09,681
And that's a specific version of the image

172
00:07:09,825 --> 00:07:12,103
that's optimized to be super, super small.

173
00:07:12,552 --> 00:07:15,615
It's an extremely low quality version of the

174
00:07:15,615 --> 00:07:17,705
image which is super small therefore,

175
00:07:18,413 --> 00:07:20,989
which you might wanna show, for example, on

176
00:07:20,989 --> 00:07:23,952
a webpage as a placeholder until the real

177
00:07:24,032 --> 00:07:24,902
image is ready.

178
00:07:25,643 --> 00:07:27,929
That is the idea. I also saved that to a

179
00:07:27,929 --> 00:07:30,424
text file here. That is that text file I

180
00:07:30,424 --> 00:07:31,181
mentioned above,

181
00:07:31,857 --> 00:07:32,421
this one here.

182
00:07:33,049 --> 00:07:35,415
And then I have some dummy HTML file being

183
00:07:35,415 --> 00:07:37,637
set up here that shows the placeholder and

184
00:07:37,734 --> 00:07:38,611
the optimized image.

185
00:07:40,349 --> 00:07:43,271
So now if I run this entire program, with

186
00:07:43,303 --> 00:07:44,572
bun run index.ts.

187
00:07:45,150 --> 00:07:47,254
It works, it finds these images, optimizes

188
00:07:47,254 --> 00:07:47,511
them all,

189
00:07:48,683 --> 00:07:50,401
and therefore in the output folder I now

190
00:07:50,514 --> 00:07:51,862
have a bunch of JPEG files.

191
00:07:52,457 --> 00:07:54,528
And especially this one here, the wildflower

192
00:07:54,528 --> 00:07:57,804
one, is now 160 kilobytes. The input image

193
00:07:57,997 --> 00:07:58,366
was

194
00:07:59,266 --> 00:08:02,638
over a megabyte, but thanks to resizing it

195
00:08:02,718 --> 00:08:05,063
and switching it from PNG to JPEG, it's now

196
00:08:05,079 --> 00:08:05,850
much smaller.

197
00:08:06,653 --> 00:08:09,045
The text files contain that Base64 image

198
00:08:09,222 --> 00:08:10,956
which you could load on a web page. And if

199
00:08:10,989 --> 00:08:12,643
you open such an HTML file

200
00:08:13,237 --> 00:08:14,923
you'll see that is the placeholder,

201
00:08:15,902 --> 00:08:17,748
but then the optimized image, as you see,

202
00:08:17,989 --> 00:08:20,317
looks crisp. And that's therefore how you

203
00:08:20,317 --> 00:08:22,453
can use the Bun Image API to load and

204
00:08:22,485 --> 00:08:23,657
transform images,

205
00:08:24,235 --> 00:08:27,222
all without installing or using any extra

206
00:08:27,222 --> 00:08:28,313
third-party packages.
